## Scope

The main conjecture `erdos_70` and the variant `omega_times_two_four` are **open research problems** — I cannot prove them. The variant `erdos_rado` ($\mathfrak{c} \to (\omega+n,4)^3_2$) is a deep theorem of Erdős–Rado whose full proof I cannot reproduce here. 

The one theorem I can prove completely is **`omega_three`**: $\mathfrak{c} \to (\omega, 3)^3_2$. I give its full proof below.

---

## Theorem (`omega_three`)

$$\mathfrak{c} \to (\omega, 3)^3_2$$

That is: for any 2-coloring (red/blue) of all 3-element subsets of the ordinal $\mathfrak{c}$ (the initial ordinal of cardinality $2^{\aleph_0}$), either there exists a red-monochromatic subset of order type $\omega$, or there exists a blue-monochromatic subset of cardinality $3$.

## Proof

Let $\alpha = \mathfrak{c}.\text{ord}$, and let $\text{isRed} : \alpha \to \alpha \to \alpha \to \text{Prop}$ be any permutation-invariant coloring of ordered triples of distinct elements, defining a 2-coloring of all 3-element subsets of $\alpha$ into red and blue. We must show:

$$\bigl(\exists\, s \subseteq \alpha,\; \operatorname{typeLT}(s) = \omega \;\wedge\; s \text{ is red-monochromatic}\bigr) \;\vee\; \bigl(\exists\, s \subseteq \alpha,\; \#s = 3 \;\wedge\; s \text{ is blue-monochromatic}\bigr).$$

We split into two cases.

### Case 1: There exists a blue triple.

Suppose there exist three distinct elements $a, b, c \in \alpha$ such that the 3-element subset $\{a, b, c\}$ is colored blue, i.e., $\neg\,\text{isRed}(a, b, c)$ (equivalently for any ordering, by permutation invariance).

Set $s = \{a, b, c\}$. Then $\#s = 3$. The only 3-element subset of $s$ is $s$ itself, and it is blue. Therefore $s$ is blue-monochromatic of cardinality $3$. This gives the second disjunct. ✓

### Case 2: No blue triple exists.

Suppose every 3-element subset of $\alpha$ is colored red. That is, for all distinct $x, y, z \in \alpha$, $\text{isRed}(x, y, z)$ holds.

Since $\mathfrak{c} = 2^{\aleph_0} \geq \aleph_0 > \aleph_0$ in particular $\mathfrak{c} \geq \aleph_0$, so $\omega \leq \mathfrak{c}$ as ordinals. Therefore there exists a subset $s \subseteq \alpha$ of order type $\omega$ — for instance, take the first $\omega$ elements of $\alpha$ (which exist since $\omega \leq \alpha$).

Now every 3-element subset of $s$ is a 3-element subset of $\alpha$, hence is colored red. Therefore $s$ is red-monochromatic with $\operatorname{typeLT}(s) = \omega$. This gives the first disjunct. ✓

### Conclusion

In both cases, one of the two alternatives holds. Therefore:

$$\mathfrak{c} \to (\omega, 3)^3_2. \qquad \blacksquare$$

---

## Remark on the remaining theorems

- **`erdos_70`** (the full conjecture $\mathfrak{c} \to (\beta, n)^3_2$ for all countable $\beta$ and $2 \le n < \omega$) and **`omega_times_two_four`** ($\mathfrak{c} \to (\omega \cdot 2, 4)^3_2$) are open research problems. I cannot prove them.

- **`erdos_rado`** ($\mathfrak{c} \to (\omega + n, 4)^3_2$) is a known theorem but its proof uses substantial machinery from the Erdős–Rado theory of partition relations for cardinals (including the "stepping-up" lemma and canonization arguments). I cannot reproduce a complete proof here.

The proof above for `omega_three` is complete and self-contained.

